  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several routes for making compound or double emulsions, where one liquid droplet becomes enclosed within another. Across the diagrams, yellow and gray droplets are dispersed in a blue continuous phase, and arrows show mixing, shear, coalescence, or phase separation steps.\n\nThe first scheme illustrates sequential emulsification: an initial emulsion is prepared, then poured into a second liquid and mixed again, producing larger droplets that contain smaller internal droplets. A second mechanism, labeled engulfment, shows nearby droplets deforming under shear and then merging so that one droplet wraps around another, forming a core–shell structure. A related route, co-emulsification, starts with two separate droplet populations that are mixed together and then emulsified simultaneously, yielding composite droplets with one or more internal inclusions.\n\nThe final part highlights phase separation as another pathway. An initially unstable single droplet can split internally after changes in conditions, such as temperature or composition, and then coarsen into a larger core–shell droplet. Similarly, many small droplets in an unstable mixture can reorganize and coarsen into multiple encapsulated droplets. Overall, the figure compares different physical routes to create structured, multi-compartment emulsions.",
